1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,246
Jump off a boat, and the boat slides back.

2
00:00:03,246 --> 00:00:06,621
Push off a friend on skates,
and you both roll away.

3
00:00:07,021 --> 00:00:10,137
Nobody pushed from outside,
yet everything moves.

4
00:00:10,587 --> 00:00:14,203
At the ice rink, Diya sees two
skaters at rest, face to face.

5
00:00:14,203 --> 00:00:15,723
They push off each other.

6
00:00:16,073 --> 00:00:18,364
One glides left, one glides right.

7
00:00:18,364 --> 00:00:20,806
Where did all this momentum come from?

8
00:00:21,306 --> 00:00:23,331
The answer is: from nowhere.

9
00:00:23,331 --> 00:00:24,398
Let's see why.

10
00:00:24,998 --> 00:00:28,341
First, a system of particles is a
group of bodies we look at together.

11
00:00:28,691 --> 00:00:31,310
Here, the system is the two skaters.

12
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:34,185
Each has a momentum: mass times velocity.

13
00:00:34,585 --> 00:00:38,985
Add them as vectors, and you get
the total momentum, capital P.

14
00:00:39,385 --> 00:00:42,358
Forces between the
skaters are inside forces.

15
00:00:42,358 --> 00:00:46,075
A push from anything else,
like a coach, is outside.

16
00:00:46,675 --> 00:00:48,541
To the motion notebook.

17
00:00:48,541 --> 00:00:52,229
Skater A is forty kilograms,
skater B is sixty.

18
00:00:52,229 --> 00:00:53,458
Both at rest.

19
00:00:53,808 --> 00:00:55,932
So the total momentum is zero.

20
00:00:55,932 --> 00:00:58,680
The gold cross marks the centre of mass.

21
00:00:59,080 --> 00:00:59,980
They push.

22
00:00:59,903 --> 00:01:02,531
A glides left at three metres per second.

23
00:01:02,531 --> 00:01:04,091
B glides right at two.

24
00:01:04,491 --> 00:01:06,925
A carries minus one hundred and twenty.

25
00:01:06,925 --> 00:01:09,313
B carries plus one hundred and twenty.

26
00:01:09,313 --> 00:01:10,939
The total is still zero.

27
00:01:11,339 --> 00:01:16,002
On the graph, A falls, B rises,
and the total stays flat on zero.

28
00:01:16,002 --> 00:01:18,073
The gold cross does not move.

29
00:01:18,573 --> 00:01:19,491
Slow-mo replay.

30
00:01:19,491 --> 00:01:21,482
Freeze in the middle of the push.

31
00:01:21,832 --> 00:01:22,798
A pushes B.

32
00:01:22,798 --> 00:01:24,071
B pushes A back.

33
00:01:24,071 --> 00:01:27,306
Equal forces, opposite
ways, for the same time.

34
00:01:27,706 --> 00:01:31,866
So whatever momentum B gets, A
gets the same, the other way.

35
00:01:31,866 --> 00:01:33,910
Inside forces cancel in pairs.

36
00:01:34,510 --> 00:01:37,040
Now they pull each other in on a rope.

37
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:39,014
Will the total momentum change?

38
00:01:39,014 --> 00:01:40,217
Pause and decide.

39
00:01:43,317 --> 00:01:44,217
No!

40
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:45,767
The pull is an inside force too.

41
00:01:45,767 --> 00:01:49,330
The total stays zero, and they
meet at the centre of mass.

42
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:52,994
Now let's write the
law, one step at a time.

43
00:01:53,344 --> 00:01:54,244
Step one.

44
00:01:54,212 --> 00:01:56,382
Add every momentum as vectors.

45
00:01:56,382 --> 00:02:00,448
P equals p one, plus p
two, and so on, up to p n.

46
00:02:01,198 --> 00:02:02,098
Step two.

47
00:02:02,059 --> 00:02:03,748
Each p is m times v.

48
00:02:03,748 --> 00:02:08,920
The sum of all the m v is MV: total
mass times the velocity of the centre

49
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:09,820
of mass.

50
00:02:10,478 --> 00:02:11,470
Step three.

51
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:17,273
How fast P changes each second, d P by
d t, equals the sum of all the forces.

52
00:02:18,023 --> 00:02:22,631
Inside forces come in equal,
opposite pairs, so they add to zero.

53
00:02:22,631 --> 00:02:24,883
Only the outside force is left.

54
00:02:25,633 --> 00:02:28,179
So d P by d t equals F external.

55
00:02:28,179 --> 00:02:33,121
With no outside force, P stays
constant, and the centre of mass keeps a

56
00:02:33,121 --> 00:02:34,367
steady velocity.

57
00:02:34,967 --> 00:02:36,020
Remember this.

58
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:40,126
Only an outside force can change
the total momentum of a system.

59
00:02:40,826 --> 00:02:43,980
Now, why does the lighter
skater glide faster?

60
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:45,068
Link by link.

61
00:02:45,418 --> 00:02:48,724
P starts at zero, and
no outside force acts.

62
00:02:48,724 --> 00:02:50,158
So it stays zero.

63
00:02:50,558 --> 00:02:55,785
So A's momentum and B's momentum must
be equal in size, and opposite in

64
00:02:55,785 --> 00:02:56,739
direction.

65
00:02:57,139 --> 00:02:59,823
Forty times three equals sixty times two.

66
00:02:59,823 --> 00:03:01,491
Less mass, more speed.

67
00:03:01,491 --> 00:03:04,343
That is why a light boat shoots back fast.

68
00:03:04,943 --> 00:03:06,102
Side by side.

69
00:03:06,102 --> 00:03:11,059
Inside forces: the skaters push each
other, and the total stays zero.

70
00:03:11,459 --> 00:03:15,759
Outside force: a coach pushes the pair
with fifty newtons for two seconds.

71
00:03:15,759 --> 00:03:17,682
The total grows to one hundred.

72
00:03:18,282 --> 00:03:19,211
Now you try.

73
00:03:19,211 --> 00:03:22,343
You sit in a cart and push
hard on its front wall.

74
00:03:22,343 --> 00:03:24,058
Will the cart roll forward?

75
00:03:24,058 --> 00:03:25,214
Pause and decide.

76
00:03:28,414 --> 00:03:29,314
No!

77
00:03:28,881 --> 00:03:31,188
You and the cart are one system.

78
00:03:31,188 --> 00:03:34,958
Your push is an inside force,
so the total stays zero.

79
00:03:35,608 --> 00:03:37,298
Now, one worked example.

80
00:03:37,648 --> 00:03:42,679
Three balls on a smooth floor: one
kilogram at six metres per second east,

81
00:03:42,679 --> 00:03:46,723
two kilograms at three west,
three kilograms at two north.

82
00:03:46,723 --> 00:03:49,695
Find P, and the centre of mass velocity.

83
00:03:50,195 --> 00:03:52,950
Along x: one times six is plus six.

84
00:03:52,950 --> 00:03:55,801
Two times three, west, is minus six.

85
00:03:55,801 --> 00:03:56,701
Zero.

86
00:03:57,149 --> 00:03:59,217
Along y: three times two.

87
00:03:59,217 --> 00:04:02,057
Six kilogram metres per second, north.

88
00:04:02,807 --> 00:04:04,491
So P is six, north.

89
00:04:04,491 --> 00:04:06,816
The total mass is six kilograms.

90
00:04:07,566 --> 00:04:09,268
V equals P over M.

91
00:04:09,268 --> 00:04:10,536
Six over six.

92
00:04:10,536 --> 00:04:12,942
One metre per second, north.

93
00:04:13,592 --> 00:04:15,054
Now, the exam trap.

94
00:04:15,404 --> 00:04:19,063
Many students think: zero total
momentum means nothing is moving.

95
00:04:19,463 --> 00:04:20,363
Not true.

96
00:04:20,326 --> 00:04:23,455
Both skaters move, but
their momenta cancel.

97
00:04:23,455 --> 00:04:27,479
Zero total means only that
the centre of mass is at rest.

98
00:04:28,129 --> 00:04:29,593
Where is this asked?

99
00:04:29,593 --> 00:04:34,065
Centre of mass velocity, explosions,
and inside versus outside forces.

100
00:04:34,664 --> 00:04:35,871
Exam level now.

101
00:04:35,871 --> 00:04:39,564
Two kilograms at three i
plus two j metres per second.

102
00:04:39,564 --> 00:04:41,773
Three kilograms at minus two j.

103
00:04:41,773 --> 00:04:43,297
A spring joins them.

104
00:04:43,297 --> 00:04:47,092
An outside force of five j
newtons acts for two seconds.

105
00:04:47,092 --> 00:04:49,147
Speed of the centre of mass?

106
00:04:49,147 --> 00:04:50,406
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:55,806 --> 00:04:57,192
The answer is B.

108
00:04:57,192 --> 00:04:58,901
Two metres per second.

109
00:04:59,401 --> 00:05:03,411
At the start, P is six i
plus four j, minus six j.

110
00:05:03,411 --> 00:05:05,528
That is six i minus two j.

111
00:05:06,028 --> 00:05:08,524
The spring is inside, so ignore it.

112
00:05:08,524 --> 00:05:10,564
The outside force adds ten j.

113
00:05:10,564 --> 00:05:12,664
P becomes six i plus eight j.

114
00:05:13,164 --> 00:05:14,511
Its size is ten.

115
00:05:14,511 --> 00:05:17,457
Divide by the total mass, five kilograms.

116
00:05:17,457 --> 00:05:19,117
Two metres per second.

117
00:05:19,717 --> 00:05:21,258
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:21,258 --> 00:05:22,840
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:27,979
Can you answer these without help?

120
00:05:28,379 --> 00:05:29,279
One.

121
00:05:28,878 --> 00:05:30,994
Two skaters at rest push apart.

122
00:05:30,994 --> 00:05:33,511
Why does the total momentum stay zero?

123
00:05:34,411 --> 00:05:35,311
Two.

124
00:05:34,849 --> 00:05:37,995
How is the total momentum
linked to the centre of mass?

125
00:05:38,895 --> 00:05:39,795
Three.

126
00:05:39,432 --> 00:05:42,595
Which forces can change the
total momentum of a system?

127
00:05:43,495 --> 00:05:46,006
Next time: turn a screw with a spanner.

128
00:05:46,006 --> 00:05:49,068
You push sideways, yet the
screw goes straight in.

129
00:05:49,068 --> 00:05:51,109
We meet the vector cross product.
